A royalty of 40 cents per ton or 8 percent of the f.o.b. mine price per ton (whichever is greater) applies to the reminder of the coal covered by the lease which is mined and shipped pursuant to future contracts. This royalty provision may be renegotiated every ten years. Other significant clauses in the amended leases, provide for:
   reclamation requirements and avoidance of environmental degradation;
2) diligent production of coal (or lease cancellation) to avoid holding of the leases for speculation;
3) preferential hiring of Indian labor;
4) an option for the Crow Tribe to acquire reclaimed surface lands held by Westmoreland Resources;
5) fixed minimum production levels;
facilities to be located on the Crow Indian Reservation or within 50 miles of the exterior boundaries of the diminished Crow Indian Reservation, exclusive of an option held at that time by Colorado Interstate Gas Company.
By June of 1982, production from Tract III must be at least 10 million tons annually, and from Tract II, 4 million tons annually.

Tract III production must reach 15 million tons annually by 1989. If these production levels are not attained, minimum royalties on the disparity between realized and stipulated production levels are to be paid to the Crow Tribe, or coal reserves must be returned to the Tribe.
